ID: B3-Ch09a-Intro
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch9_assassinate,0)
set(v_b3_ch9_cover,0)
set(v_b3_ch9_enemies,0)
set(v_b3_ch9_hadrik,0)
set(v_b3_ch9_hydra,0)
set(v_b3_ch9_listen,0)
set(v_b3_ch9_teleport,0)
set(v_is_dead,0)


"Albert," the king of the underground calls out across the drafty floor of the warehouse. "What do you see out there?"

Albert pushes through the heavy timber doors, water sheeting from his leather cloak and pooling over the cracked flagstones. Rain hammers the corrugated iron roof with a deafening, continuous roar, thunder rattling the high rafters.

"The gale is on our side, my lord," Albert says, shaking the soaked hood back from his brow. "The brass spheres are struggling against the updrafts. They wobble when the wind shears, and their turns are wide and sluggish."

"Rudimentary air enchantments on the rotors, just as I suspected," the underground king says, tapping a gloved finger against his chin. "The ancient lessathi had no reason to grant maintenance automata the complex current-weaving needed to ride out a tempest. Am I using the proper term? 'Drones'?"

"That is what Billy Bob called them," I say.

Peter steps forward, arms folded across his leather brigandine, rain already dripping from his cuffs. "My king, engaging a crippled foe is sound tactics, but are we wise to commit before the full garrison has assembled?"

"Varathian summer tempests break as swiftly as they form," the king replies, his voice carrying clearly over the drumming overhead. "A window like this will not present itself twice. We strike now, even if our numbers fall short of the ideal."

Albert scans the drafty expanse of the warehouse, counting silhouettes against the dust-streaked windows. "Has Breynan finished the transfers? I do not see Herman or Howard."

"Howard remains below," the king says, his jaw set. "Yesterday's wound opened the moment you crossed to the surface, despite the white magic we poured into it. I ordered him to stand down."

"A bitter pill for him," Albert murmurs. "He lived for the day we marched on Ollendor's square. And Herman?"

"Herman arrives with the final detachment," the underground king says. "I instructed Breynan to space each transit by several minutes. We cannot risk frying the transfer array—not with our chief artificer locked in a cell for treason."

A young rebel mage standing near our group raises a hand, shifting his weight. "Will Breynan join the vanguard, my lord?"

"No," the king says firmly. "Breynan remains below. If the strike falters, our people will need a hand on the helm. You have trained under him for years, Jack, and you are among the sharpest blades we have. I expect you to hold the line without him and do his teachings credit."

The young mage straightens his spine, fingers brushing his scabbard. "Yes, sir. I will."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Intro2
TEXT:

 A brilliant flare of white light washes over the empty crates and broken rafters, humming with the ozone tang of teleportation. When the radiance collapses, twenty armed rebels stand in formation across the floor, weapons drawn and cloaks dry.

"Is this the last of them?" Peter asks.

"All accounted for," the underground king says. "Save for Howard."

He climbs onto an overturned packing crate, projecting his voice over the rumble of thunder shaking the walls.

"Lend me your ears!"

The low murmur among the ranks dies down. A hundred men and women turn toward their sovereign, breath misting in the cold drafts sweeping under the doors.

"I will not waste breath on grand speeches," the king calls out. "You know what we fight for. You have lived in the dark for decades, waiting for Ollendor to breathe free. One obstacle remains between our people and the sky: the Overseer. A machine leftover from an era of iron chains and silent graves. That era ends today. Today we take back what was stolen from our fathers. Today, Ollendor is ours!"

A ragged cheer surges through the warehouse, iron clashing against iron shields. The king raises both palms, bringing instant silence back to the room.

"You know your assignments," he says. "The plaza lies two streets north. We break every drone that crosses our path. May the gods steady your hands."

The rebels filter out into the gale in organized squads. Albert steps over to Jack, resting a heavy hand on the young mage's shoulder.

"Steady, Jack?"

"Y-yes, sir!"

"Breynan taught you well," Albert says. "If the field turns ugly, find my back. Understood?"

"Yes, sir! I will not let you down!"

Albert offers a brief nod and strides toward the door behind the king. Jack lingers by a timber post. He reaches into his tunic, pulls out a silver locket, and presses it between white-knuckled palms with his eyes shut tight.

choice("Ask Jack if he got the pendant from someone important to him.", B3-Ch09a-Jack, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Jack, v_b3_ch9_jack = 0)
choice("Reassure Jack that we'll all make it out alive.", B3-Ch09a-Jack, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Jack, v_b3_ch9_jack = 1)
choice("Don't tell Jack anything.", B3-Ch09a-Jack, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Jack, v_b3_ch9_jack = 2)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Jack
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_jack == 0) {
"Did someone important give you that locket?" I ask him quietly.

Jack blinks, startled, hastily tucking the silver piece against his chest beneath his damp tunic. "Oh... Yes. My wife. When the panic starts creeping up my throat before a skirmish, I hold this and remember her face. It anchors me. Reminds me what I have to return to."

"Move it, Jack!" a squad leader barks from the threshold. "Form up!"

"See you on the cobblestones," Jack says, offering a quick salute before dashing out into the rain.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_jack == 1) {
"Keep your head down," I tell him. "We are all walking out of this."

Jack blinks, startled to find me watching, then forces a tight, determined smile. "Right. Here is to surviving the day."

"Move it, Jack!" a squad leader barks from the threshold. "Form up!"

"See you on the cobblestones," Jack says, offering a quick salute before dashing out into the rain.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_jack == 2) {
"Move it, Jack!" a squad leader barks from the threshold. "Form up!"

Jack flinches, shoving the locket back under his tunic as he catches sight of the lingering squads. "Right! Moving!"

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 && v_b3_ch9_jack != 2) {
"Cold feet, Barry?" Daren murmurs at my elbow, watching the young rebel slip into the downpour.

He means our secret bargain to back the Overseer and throw the king to the wolves. I check the corners of the room before answering in a harsh whisper.

"No. We are not sentencing them to slaughter, Daren. Once the dust settles, they march right back underground where they belong."

"That depends entirely on how messy the clash gets," Daren says, tightening his gauntlet straps until the leather groans. "We keep the body count as close to zero as humanly possible."

"So," Hadrik grunts, stepping up beside us as the last of the vanguard vanishes into the deluge. "Any grand words before we jump into the meat grinder?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0 || v_b3_ch9_jack == 2) {
"So," Hadrik grunts, stepping up beside us as the last of the vanguard vanishes into the deluge. "Any grand words before we jump into the meat grinder?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 0 && v_b3_ch6_kelrim == 1 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 0 && v_b3_ch6_plan == 3) {
"One thing," I say. "Rose reached me through the transceiver before the jump. She ran herself ragged drilling her time magic yesterday and woke up barely able to stand. We fight without her today."

"Fair enough," Daren says with a solemn nod. "She has carried her share of our burdens. This scrap belongs to us."

"Kelrim is absent as well," Kate observes, her eyes fixed on the empty doorway. "He sounded determined to march on Ollendor yesterday."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_kelrim == 1 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 0 || v_b3_ch6_plan == 3 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 0) {
"Kelrim is absent," Kate observes, her eyes fixed on the empty doorway. "He sounded determined to march on Ollendor yesterday."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy == 0 && v_b3_ch6_kelrim != 1 && v_b3_ch6_plan != 3) {
"One thing," I say. "Rose reached me through the transceiver before the jump. She ran herself ragged drilling her time magic yesterday and woke up barely able to stand. We fight without her today."

"Fair enough," Daren says with a solemn nod. "She has carried her share of our burdens. This scrap belongs to us."

}
#if(v_b3_ch4_billy != 0 && v_b3_ch6_kelrim != 1 && v_b3_ch6_plan != 3) {
"We settled the details last night," Kate says, cutting through the silence. "Second-guessing a solid plan thirty seconds before contact only gets people killed."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Delay, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Delay, v_b3_ch9_jack = 2)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Delay
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_kelrim != 1 && v_b3_ch6_plan != 3) {
Melindra wanders over from the far crates, shaking rainwater from her fingers. "Listen up. I poked my head outside while you were debating philosophy, and that tempest is vile. Barry, if I hand you direct control of my flight currents like usual, the gale will rip you sideways. I can anchor the draft from down here to keep you stable, but you will feel a lag whenever you bank or dive. How does that sound?"

"How much of a lag?" I ask.

"A second or two," Melindra says, twirling a lock of hair around her finger. "Maybe more if I lose line of sight, or if some drone tries to turn my skull into an ashtray."

"And if you get knocked out while I am twenty feet in the air?" I say. "Do I hover like a stuffed duck?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_kelrim == 1 && v_b3_ch8_king != 5 || v_b3_ch6_plan == 3 && v_b3_ch8_king != 5) {
"I spoke with him before we mustered at the array," Daren says quietly. "The king turned him away. He insisted on seasoned fighters only, arguing Kelrim does more good tending the wounded when the squads limp back below. The lad only came to the platform to see us off."

"Understood," Kate says.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_kelrim == 1 && v_b3_ch8_king == 5 || v_b3_ch6_plan == 3 && v_b3_ch8_king == 5) {
"I spoke with him before we mustered at the array," Daren says quietly. "The king turned him away. He insisted on seasoned fighters only, arguing Kelrim does more good tending the wounded when the squads limp back below. The lad only came to the platform to see us off."

"Surprising that the king turned him down in person," Kate says. "A chaotic street battle would have been the cleanest way to arrange an unfortunate accident for the goblin—and for us."

"I had the same thought," Daren admits. "I told Kelrim to shadow Breynan until we return, just in case. But murdering him in the bunker carries too much political risk for the king right now."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_kelrim == 1 || v_b3_ch6_plan == 3) {
Melindra wanders over from the far crates, shaking rainwater from her fingers. "Listen up. I poked my head outside while you were debating philosophy, and that tempest is vile. Barry, if I hand you direct control of my flight currents like usual, the gale will rip you sideways. I can anchor the draft from down here to keep you stable, but you will feel a lag whenever you bank or dive. How does that sound?"

"How much of a lag?" I ask.

"A second or two," Melindra says, twirling a lock of hair around her finger. "Maybe more if I lose line of sight, or if some drone tries to turn my skull into an ashtray."

"And if you get knocked out while I am twenty feet in the air?" I say. "Do I hover like a stuffed duck?"

}
"You keep partial steering," Melindra says, "though the flow will fight you, and you might have to repeat a gesture three times before the air catches. If you want the raw version with zero lag, I can cast that instead, but flying with full steering in this gale is like riding a gnomish gyrocopter through a cyclone."

"I flew a plane through an electrical storm once," I tell her. "I nearly cracked the landing gear and almost died nine different ways, but the wheels touched dirt."

"Suit yourself," Melindra says with a shrug. "Once we step out into the rain, the weave is locked. Make your choice now."

choice(""Fine, I'll have the stable air currents then, please."", B3-Ch09a-Currents, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Currents, v_b3_ch9_currents = 1)
choice(""It's okay, you can cast the same air currents as always. I'll handle it."", B3-Ch09a-Currents, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Currents, v_b3_ch9_currents = 0)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Currents
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_currents == 0) {
"It's all right, you can cast the same air currents as always. I'll handle it."

"Your funeral," Melindra says. She extends both palms toward me, her brow furrowing as she channels a steady pulse of magical energy from her fingertips.

Within seconds, the air around my limbs thickens, churning in tight spirals that respond instantly to the tilt of my wrists. The updraft remains faint, coiled around my ankles and waiting for the upward sweep of my arms to launch me skyward.

Leila traces glowing runes in the air between us: "The vanguard left minutes ago. Should we move to support them?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_currents == 1) {
"Fine, I'll have the stable air currents then, please."

"Sensible," Melindra says. She extends both palms toward me, her brow furrowing as she channels a steady pulse of magical energy from her fingertips.

The draft settles around my shoulders with a heavy, stubborn density, far stiffer than her usual weave. It resists minor twitches, locked to an invisible tether anchored to her position. I will have to muscle my way through turns while dodging lightning.

Leila traces glowing runes in the air between us: "The vanguard left minutes ago. Should we move to support them?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"No," Daren says, checking his scabbard. "The king ordered us to hold back while they erect ward perimeters. He wants the Overseer blind to our numbers so the machine misjudges the initial defense."

"Or he does not trust us at his back and wants us out of the fray," Kate says.

"If he wants to leave his best fighters on the bench, that is his blunder," Daren says. "We give them another minute. If no runner arrives, we scout the square ourselves."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
"No," Daren says, checking his scabbard. "The king ordered us to hold back while they erect ward perimeters. He wants the Overseer blind to our numbers so the machine misjudges the initial defense."

"Or he does not trust us at his back and wants us out of the fray," Kate says.

"If they already suspect foul play, defying orders will wreck the ambush before it starts," Daren counters. "The Overseer will not trigger the trap until our group is in place. The king will summon us once he sees the square remain quiet."

}
Right on cue, a drenched rebel runner raps on the warehouse door, shouting that the king requests our presence. We step out into the blinding deluge. The wind howls down the stone alleyways, driving sheets of freezing rain against our cloaks as we follow the guide toward the central plaza.

A hundred underground fighters hold the open flagstones. Roughly ten mages chant in tight circles, weaving dome wards over the perimeter, while the rest stand in rank with loaded crossbows, shields locked, staring up into the storm. Hovering high above the eaves, dozens of bronze drones drift lazily in the gale, ignoring the armed army occupying a public square during a mandatory quarantine.

"Clear a path," the underground king commands as we approach. "I will hear what they have to say."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Gregory, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Gregory)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Gregory
TEXT:

The rebel ranks part, clearing a corridor across the wet flagstones. Peter and Albert flank their sovereign like stone gargoyles, rain running off their shoulders in steady streams. The king has to shout over the roar of the gale.

"I will dispense with courtesy," he calls out. "Tell me what you make of this."

"You mean why the sky is quiet?" Daren answers, wiping water from his brow. "The machine might be baiting your vanguard into the open."

"A plausible theory," the king says. "Yet my forward scouts report no mechanical movement across the northern thoroughfares. One would expect the Overseer to marshal defenses, even if he prefers to let us initiate. Instead, the drones idle above the rooftops as if we were common travelers. I find such indifference troubl—"

A sharp click cuts him off. The transceiver at his belt crackles with static.

"Your highness! Can you hear me?"

The king taps the rune with his thumb. "Report, Gregory."

"It is just as you foresaw, my lord!" the scout shouts over the hiss of line interference. "The moment the outsiders cleared the warehouse threshold, the brass spheres pivoted and began a slow crawl toward the plaza! They have been in league with the Overseer from the beginning! There is no doubt!"

An angry mutter ripples through the surrounding rebels. Crossbows shift; grips tighten on sword hilts. The king presses the heel of his hand to his forehead, letting out a sharp breath.

"And what were your standing orders should the enemy advance, Gregory?"

"To... to hold position until summoned, my lord. Because you intended to hear the outsiders first." Gregory hesitates, then presses on. "Forgive me, sire, but the timing is damning! Why waste breath on traitors?"

"That judgment belongs to me, scout, not you," the king snaps. "Return to your watch. I am closing the channel."

He cuts the transmission with a sharp tap of his knuckle, then fixes us with a steady, unblinking stare.

"Well? Do you have an explanation for my scout's report?"

choice("Tell the king of the underground that he and Gregory are just jumping to conclusions.", B3-Ch09a-Jumping, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Jumping, v_b3_ch9_conclusions = 0)
choice("Tell him that we were contacted by the Overseer but refused to join his side.", B3-Ch09a-Refused, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Refused, v_b3_ch9_conclusions = 1)
choice("Tell him that we're planning to join the Overseer's side in this fight.", B3-Ch09a-Caught, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Caught, v_b3_ch9_conclusions = 2)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Jumping
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"You and Gregory are leaping at shadows," I say. "The Overseer calculated his force requirement based on numbers. He waited to see if our group would commit to the plaza before sending enough metal to wipe us all out."

"You continue to conceal your dealings through the blue resonance crystals," the underground king says, his voice flat. "Conspiracy against our cause remains the simplest explanation."

He knows about the crystal transmissions. Did an underground sentry listen at the tunnel vents?

"Hold on," Daren cuts in, raising an open gauntlet. "The Overseer did reach out. He offered us terms to ambush your forces, and we flatly rejected him."

"If that were true," the king says, "your companion would not have played the fool just now. He would have led with the truth."

"We withheld information because you gave us cause to doubt you," Daren shoots back. "The Overseer broadcast a private recording of you instructing Albert to quietly dispose of us if we opposed your coronation. You can hardly blame us for keeping our cards close."

The king exhales slowly, rain dripping from the brim of his leather cap. "So that reached your ears. In that case, your caution is understandable. We have no time to dissect politics in a downpour. I accept your account for now. We settle the rest once Ollendor is secure."

He strikes the transceiver at his hip.

"Gregory. How far out is the swarm?"

"Descending on your flank right now, my lord!"

"Excellent," the king says. "Let us give them an irresistible lure."

He throws both arms wide. The air beneath his boots roars, lifting him smoothly off the flagstones as a fork of blue lightning splits the clouds above the rooftops.

"He flies?" Hadrik bellows, grinning into the storm.

"I borrowed the principle from your friend Barry!" the king shouts down through the wind. "My air mages adapted Melindra's current weave after my scouts described his maneuvers in the streets!"

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
"You and Gregory are leaping at shadows," I say. "The Overseer calculated his force requirement based on numbers. He waited to see if our group would commit to the plaza before sending enough metal to wipe us all out."

"You continue to conceal your dealings through the blue resonance crystals," the underground king says, his voice flat. "Conspiracy against our cause remains the simplest explanation."

He knows about the crystal transmissions. Did an underground sentry listen at the tunnel vents?

"More baseless accusations," I say, keeping my tone dry. "Do you have any actual evidence of collusion, or just wild conjecture?"

"I have heard enough," the underground king says, glancing toward his lieutenants. "Albert. Peter. Keep weapons drawn on them. If any of them twitches toward a spell or a blade, cut them down. I will handle the vanguard."

"Alone, my lord?" Albert asks, his eyes wide.

"I have a theory to test," the king says.

He strikes the transceiver at his hip.

"Gregory. How far out is the swarm?"

"Descending on your flank right now, my lord!"

"Excellent," the king says. "Let us give them an irresistible lure."

He throws both arms wide. The air beneath his boots roars, lifting him smoothly off the flagstones as a fork of blue lightning splits the clouds above the rooftops.

"He flies?" Hadrik bellows, grinning into the storm.

"I borrowed the principle from your friend Barry!" the king shouts down through the wind. "My air mages adapted Melindra's current weave after my scouts described his maneuvers in the streets!"

}
He darts in tight arcs through the gale, fighting a sudden wind shear for a split second before his aura hardens, locking his flight path back into balance.

"The steering lacks finesse, but manual aura pressure compensates well enough," he calls down. "Now... let them come."

He ascends into the driving rain, his silhouette blurring against the dark clouds. Within seconds, crimson optical arrays flare across the upper sky. Dozens of Overseer drones converge on his altitude.

The king holds his ground without retaliating, projecting a dense sphere of hardened aura that deflects both the deluge and the barrage of elemental bolts tearing through the clouds. More drones peel off from the surrounding roofs, swarming the shimmering globe in a frantic, humming cluster.

He lets them gather for a solid minute, drawing nearly a hundred brass hulls into a tight formation around his barrier. Just as the swarm blots him out entirely, an incandescent shockwave detonates outward from the sphere, shattering every drone in the cluster in a single blinding blast.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Carcasses, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Carcasses)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Refused
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch9_conclusions,0) if (v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 && v_bluff >= 3 || v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0)
set(v_b3_ch9_conclusions,3) if (v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 && v_bluff < 3)
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"There is something we kept to ourselves," I say.

"Speak," the king replies, his posture rigid.

"The Overseer contacted us through the blue crystals back in the bunker," I tell him. "He offered us terms to set up an ambush against your vanguard. We refused."

The king glances to his left. "Albert. Is he speaking truthfully?"

Albert watches me with narrowed eyes. "The aura remains steady, my lord. He is not lying."

"Then why withhold this until our blades are drawn?" the king demands. "How do you expect us to fight alongside allies who bury critical intelligence?"

"Because you were burying secrets of your own," Daren snaps back. "The Overseer played us an audio recording of you telling Albert to quietly eliminate us if we balked at your right to rule Ollendor. We had good reason to keep our distance."

The king exhales slowly, rain dripping from the brim of his leather cap. "So that reached your ears. In that case, your caution is understandable. We have no time to dissect politics in a downpour. I accept your account for now. We settle the rest once Ollendor is secure."

}
