ID: B3-Ch09a-Caught
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"Fine, you caught us," I say. "We've been talking to the Overseer in secret and made plans to ambush you."

"A confession, then," the king says, his face hardening.

"Wait, Barry is bluffing! We never—" Daren shouts, but the king cuts him off with a slice of his hand.

"I have heard enough," the king says. "Albert. Peter. Keep weapons drawn on them. If any of them twitches toward a spell or a blade, cut them down. I will handle the vanguard."

"Alone, my lord?" Albert asks, his eyes wide.

"I have a theory to test," the king says.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
"Fine, you caught us," I say. "We've been talking to the Overseer in secret and made plans to ambush you."

"A confession, then," the king says, his face hardening.

"Wait, listen to me—" Daren tries to interject, but the king cuts him off with a sharp gesture.

"I have heard enough," the king says. "Albert. Peter. Keep weapons drawn on them. If any of them twitches toward a spell or a blade, cut them down. I will handle the vanguard."

"Alone, my lord?" Albert asks, his eyes wide.

"I have a theory to test," the king says.

}
He strikes the transceiver at his hip.

"Gregory. How far out is the swarm?"

"Descending on your flank right now, my lord!"

"Excellent," the king says. "Let us give them an irresistible lure."

He throws both arms wide. The air beneath his boots roars, lifting him smoothly off the flagstones as a fork of blue lightning splits the clouds above the rooftops.

"He flies?" Hadrik bellows, grinning into the storm.

"I borrowed the principle from your friend Barry!" the king shouts down through the wind. "My air mages adapted Melindra's current weave after my scouts described his maneuvers in the streets!"

He darts in tight arcs through the gale, fighting a sudden wind shear for a split second before his aura hardens, locking his flight path back into balance.

"The steering lacks finesse, but manual aura pressure compensates well enough," he calls down. "Now... let them come."

He ascends into the driving rain, his silhouette blurring against the dark clouds. Within seconds, crimson optical arrays flare across the upper sky. Dozens of Overseer drones converge on his altitude.

The king holds his ground without retaliating, projecting a dense sphere of hardened aura that deflects both the deluge and the barrage of elemental bolts tearing through the clouds. More drones peel off from the surrounding roofs, swarming the shimmering globe in a frantic, humming cluster.

He lets them gather for a solid minute, drawing nearly a hundred brass hulls into a tight formation around his barrier. Just as the swarm blots him out entirely, an incandescent shockwave detonates outward from the sphere, shattering every drone in the cluster in a single blinding blast.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Carcasses, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Carcasses)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Carcasses
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch9_premonition,0) if (v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0)
set(v_b3_ch9_premonition,2) if (v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 && v_premonition < 4)
set(v_b3_ch9_premonition,1) if (v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 && v_premonition >= 4)
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0 && v_b3_ch9_conclusions == 2) {
"Incoming!" Daren roars.

Shards of burning bronze and shattered rotors rain down across the flagstones. Rebels scramble for the shelter of building eaves, shields raised to deflect sizzling debris. High above, a fresh formation of drones drops through the cloud base to engage the king, while thirty more dive toward the plaza floor.

"We take the low swarm first," I call out. "The king has the upper tier well in hand."

"We are pinned where we stand," Daren grunts, his heavy shield locked before him. "You convinced the entire vanguard we are enemy agents with that stunt. They will cut us down the moment we draw steel."

"They need drones destroyed more than they need a grudge match," I say.

I glance toward Peter, who stands five paces away under the overhang, arms crossed over his chest, scowling beneath his soaked hood.

"You object to us scrapping a few machines, Peter?"

Peter offers only silence and a murderous glare.

"See?" I tell Daren. "No objections. Move!"

We slip past Peter's guard and engage the forward drones before they reach the rebel line. Our weapons barely taste metal before every drone in the plaza suddenly halts in mid-air. Their rotors whine as they ascend ten paces, spinning on their axes while their red optical slits pulse in rapid, rhythmic flashes.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0 && v_b3_ch9_conclusions != 2) {
"Incoming!" Daren roars.

Shards of burning bronze and shattered rotors rain down across the flagstones. Rebels scramble for the shelter of building eaves, shields raised to deflect sizzling debris. High above, a fresh formation of drones drops through the cloud base to engage the king, while thirty more dive toward the plaza floor.

"We take the low swarm first," I call out. "The king has the upper tier well in hand."

"Agreed," Daren shouts over the rain. "Hold formation! Nobody dies today!"

We push forward alongside the rebel line, blades ready, but the frontline drones suddenly brake in mid-descent. They pitch upward, spinning in tight vertical loops as their crimson lenses blink in rapid, synchronized pulses.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 && v_premonition < 4) {
"Now!" Daren bellows as burning wreckage hammers the flagstones.

He slams his shield into the nearest rebel guard, sending the man sprawling across the wet stone, and draws his blade to engage the squad leaders. We surge forward to support him.

Mid-stride, Kate's back arches with violent rigidity. A sharp tremor ripples through her spine, and her knees buckle beneath her. She hits the cobblestones face-first and does not move.

No attacker stands behind her. Invisibility and an electric shock prod. Albert.

"Look alive!" I yell, stepping over her fallen frame with my dagger drawn. "Albert is masked and using an electric weapon! Watch your backs!"

"Did he drop Kate?" Hadrik roars, stomping toward us through the rain. "Point him out! I cannot break local bones under the rules, but I will pin him to the dirt until you can disarm him!"

"Keep moving!" I shout back. "Scan the rain for footprints!"

Hadrik drops to one knee beside Kate, checking her throat. "Pulse is steady. Knocked cold. Better to leave her under for now than risk an uneven revival."

"Reinforcements above!" I point to thirty red-eyed drones swooping low over the rooftops toward the rebel flank. "The king is tied down in the clouds! We hit them while—"

The descending drones brake simultaneously. They lift straight up into the storm, spinning on their axes as their crimson eyes flicker in unison.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1 && v_premonition >= 4) {
"Now!" Daren bellows as burning wreckage hammers the flagstones.

He slams his shield into the nearest rebel guard, sending the man sprawling across the wet stone, and draws his blade to engage the squad leaders. We surge forward to support him.

As Daren gives the signal, a violent premonition hits my mind: Kate's spine arching in agony, electricity arcing silently across her shoulders, her body collapsing onto the slick cobblestones.

An invisible strike from behind. Albert.

"Kate!" I roar. "Behind you! Albert—freeze the air!"

Kate does not hesitate. She spins, frost exploding outward from her outstretched palms in an instant dome. A sculpture of ice crystallizes out of empty air—Albert caught mid-stride with an electric discharge baton raised in his grip.

"Flower! The thaw drill from yesterday!" Kate shouts.

"On it!" Flower bounds forward, summoning a swirling vortex of a hundred miniature fireballs that orbit the frozen infiltrator at blistering speed.

The heat intensifies until the flames merge into a blinding ring of white-hot air. The ice encasing Albert flash-melts into water and steam, pooling over his boots. As the fiery ring dissipates, Albert crumples onto the wet stones, limp and unconscious.

"Is he breathing?" Kate asks, chest heaving.

Flower presses two fingers to his neck. "Heart is beating fine! Just out cold!"

"The counter-measure holds," Kate breathes, shaking frost from her wrists.

"Reinforcements above!" I point toward thirty red-eyed drones diving on the rebel flank. "The king is occupied in the clouds! We hit them while—"

The descending drones brake simultaneously. They lift straight up into the storm, spinning on their axes as their crimson eyes flicker in unison.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Warning, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Warning, v_b3_ch3_stats_unavailable = 1)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Warning
TEXT:

"Warning. Warning. Warning."

The synthetic drone voices chant in perfect unison, high, metallic, and resonant, as the blue crystals embedded in their casings burn with sudden, blinding intensity.

"Critical casualty threshold exceeded within designated sector. Initiating emergency containment protocol. Stand by."

"Emergency protocol?" Hadrik barks, swinging his axe in the deluge. "What in the seven hells is that supposed to—"

The world snaps out of existence.

Sound dies first. Hadrik's bellow, the shriek of the storm, the thunderous clash of steel and spells—all cut to absolute, suffocating silence. The dark city, the rain, the flagstones beneath my boots vanish into a void of pure, featureless white radiance.

I drift in an ocean of light. No buildings. No cobblestones. No companions.

When I look down, I find nothing. No boots, no trousers, no chest, no arms. There is no weight, no skin, no heartbeat. My eyes must exist—the glare registers with searing clarity—but I am reduced to a disembodied point of perception tumbling through an endless sea of illumination.

Then the terror hits: my chest is not rising.

No air passes over my tongue. No breath expands in my throat. Am I suffocating? If I do not pull air into my lungs right now, will my consciousness black out and dissolve?

choice("Attempt to breathe.", B3-Ch09a-Breathe, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Breathe, v_ac_b3_ch9_consolation = +1)
choice("Don't breathe.", B3-Ch09a-Calm, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Calm)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Breathe
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1)
I try to draw air into my chest, but my throat is an empty abstraction. The panic spikes instantly. I strain, trying to heave nonexistent ribs and force oxygen down phantom airways.

The harder I fight for breath, the sharper the agony becomes. My mind demands the mechanics of living flesh, frantic to feed lungs that are not there, choking on a reflex it cannot fulfill.

The light blurs. Thought crumbles under the phantom pressure, and the void swallows what remains of my awareness.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Calm
TEXT:

Stop. Think it through.

I have no ribs, no diaphragm, no trachea. If I were dying of asphyxiation, the panic would have killed me ten seconds ago. There is no air here because there are no lungs to fill. I am still conscious, which means oxygen is irrelevant.

The pervasive, weightless glare stirs a memory. I felt this exact texture of mind-numbing light when the Magium possessed the Overseer and dragged my soul across the boundaries of reality.

The ethereal plane.

The blue crystals ejected us from our physical bodies. Are Daren, Kate, and the others scattered across this radiance as well? I cannot float here waiting to dissolve. I need to move. I need traction. I need... hands.

The instant the concept forms, two pale hands materialize before me in the glare. I flex the fingers. The knuckles bend; the tendons pull in perfect sync with my will. Two detached wrists floating in empty brilliance.

Thought equals form.

If desire creates limbs, I want legs. Give me legs.

I wait. The white expanse remains empty. Nothing sprouts beneath my vantage. I concentrate harder, picturing calves, knees, boots.

Still nothing.

Fine. Hands will have to do. I cup my palms and pull against the ambient light in a steady breaststroke. There are no landmarks to gauge distance, but a subtle shift in the luminescence suggests forward momentum.

"Barry? Barry, is that you?"

Flower's voice carries across the void, clear and buoyant. A small figure cuts through the brightness, closing the distance at alarming speed.

"Hold still! I've got you!"

She skids to a halt in front of me. Her eyes burn a vivid, molten crimson, and the aura rolling off her frame radiates fierce, palpable heat.

"Look at you!" Flower beams, clapping her hands. "You made yourself hands! That is amazing!"

"Never mind the hands," I say. "Your eyes are glowing red, and your aura feels like a furnace. Are you alright?"

"Oh, that?" She waves a dismissive hand. "That is just how I look now!"

She snaps her fingers. Sheets of orange flame erupt around her body, hovering exactly one inch off her skin without charring her tunic or hair.

"See?" Flower says, extinguishing the blaze with a flick of her wrist. "Completely fine!"

"If you say so," I mutter. "How do you have a complete body while I am stuck as a pair of floating gloves? What am I missing?"

"I was born here," Flower says. "I have never not had a body in the ether. If you want limbs, you just have to... imagine them harder! Making things out of nothing is easy here. Watch!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Tree, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Tree)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Tree
TEXT:

She points into the empty glare. A towering date palm bursts into existence, leaves rustling in an unfelt breeze. With a snap of her fingers, the palm tree morphs into a wooden merchant cart, which instantly compresses into a massive granite boulder. She claps once, and the rock vanishes without a trace.

"Incredible," I breathe. "Can you teach me the sequence?"

"Umm... probably not," Flower admits, scratching her cheek. "Petal always says I am terrible at explaining things to people. It is just instinct for me. Being an ethereal helps, I guess! But we cannot leave you out in the open. The glare erodes raw souls. Grab my hand—I will take you to my house! Leila and Hadrik are already there. Petal too!"

She catches my conjured fingers. Flames wash over both of us—warm rather than burning—and a flash of violet radiance envelops my vision, pulling us through the light at dizzying speed.

"Barry! You made it!"

Leila's inner voice echoes directly into my thoughts, rich, clear, and melodious, just as it sounded during our last journey into the ether.

The white void gives way to a sprawling, high-ceilinged room cluttered with carved wooden toys, vivid tapestries depicting knights slaying hydras, and a polished oak side table supporting a porcelain vase filled with fresh tulips. Leila and Hadrik stand beside the hearth, solid and fully formed. Illuna is nowhere in sight.

"Told you, Leila!" Flower chirps, bouncing on her heels. "Barry didn't do anything reckless, like trying to breathe without a chest!"

My conjured fingers twitch. "What was that about suffocating?"

"When I retrieved Leila," Flower explains, "I told her that displaced souls usually drift in a daze until someone collects them. The only real danger is if someone tries to draw breath, because the mind panics when it realizes there are no lungs or air, and the shock can snuff out their consciousness. Leila panicked immediately. She said you overanalyze everything and were guaranteed to choke yourself on logic. But you didn't do that, right?"

"Well," I say, clearing my throat. "I was about two seconds away from suffocating until I reasoned that I didn't need oxygen to begin with."

"I knew it!" Leila's exasperated thought rings in my mind. "Even while being unmade by the Magium, he was dissecting whether my hand was an illusion!"

Hadrik lets out a booming laugh that rattles the toy shelves.

"Classic Barry! Turning a simple stroll through the afterlife into a near-death duel with his own brain! Though hearing Leila speak directly into my skull is still throwing me for a loop. You heard her voice before, right, Barry?"

"Once, during our first brush with the ether," I say. "Which brings me to my next question: why am I the only one here reduced to a pair of floating paws? How did you two manifest full bodies?"

"I provided the necessary instruction," a calm, unfamiliar voice speaks from the archway.

I turn toward the sound. A woman stands in the doorway, appearing in her late twenties, with sharp elven ears, sapphire-blue hair, bright blue eyes, and a simple indigo dress. She stands roughly my height, watching me with a cold, analytical frown. Despite the unfamiliar frame, the severe posture and critical glare are instantly recognizable.

choice(""Illuna, is that you?"", B3-Ch09a-Feather, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Feather, v_b3_ch9_illuna = 0)
choice(""Hey, you look pretty good for an elf spirit that is several hundred years old!"", B3-Ch09a-Feather, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Feather, v_b3_ch9_illuna = 1)
choice(""Wow, I was right. Your blue eyes really do make you look fat!"", B3-Ch09a-Feather, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Feather, v_b3_ch9_illuna = 2)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Feather
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_illuna == 0) {
"Illuna, is that you?"

"Were you anticipating four feet of height, red pigtails, and bare feet?" Illuna replies dryly.

"To be fair, yes," I say. "After months of traveling, picturing you as anything other than Flower with blue eyes takes some mental adjustment."

"A common complaint," Illuna says. "Hadrik and Leila required several minutes to process the distinction as well."

"So... you know how to rebuild a body?"

"Hold still," Illuna says.

She conjures a white raven feather in her palm and holds it out.

"Take this. Fix your gaze on the quill and recite the incantation I give you. Maintain absolute focus on the object and enunciate clearly. Do you understand?"

"Ready," I say, steadying my floating hands around the quill. "Give me the phrase."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_illuna == 1) {
"Hey, you look pretty good for an elf spirit that is several hundred years old!"

"Sarcasm noted, though three centuries is hardly venerable for an elven spirit," Illuna replies evenly. "Appearing in my twenties by human standards is perfectly conventional."

"I have not spent much time around elven nobility, so I will take your word for it," I say. "Can you help me manifest a torso?"

"Hold still," Illuna says.

She conjures a white raven feather in her palm and holds it out.

"Take this. Fix your gaze on the quill and recite the incantation I give you. Maintain absolute focus on the object and enunciate clearly. Do you understand?"

"Ready," I say, steadying my floating hands around the quill. "Give me the phrase."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_illuna == 2) {
"Wow, I was right. Your blue eyes really do make you look fat!"

Illuna's brow arches with glacial disdain. "Fascinating choice of words for someone whose existence currently depends on my instruction. Do you prefer spending the rest of this engagement as a pair of floating gloves?"

"My mistake," I say quickly. "I take it back. I would like my torso returned, please."

"Take this," Illuna says, conjuring a white raven feather and passing it to my hands.

"Fix your gaze on the quill and recite the incantation I give you. Maintain absolute focus on the object and enunciate clearly. Do you understand?"

"Ready," I say, steadying my floating hands around the quill. "Give me the phrase."

}
"Repeat after me," Illuna commands. "Sehok amon rik tus selem."

"Sehok... amon rik tus... selem!" I recite, staring unblinkingly at the barbs of the feather.

The moment the final syllable leaves my thoughts, pale lines of light trace down from my wrists. Forearms, shoulders, chest, and legs weave into solid reality in a matter of seconds. I look down at my boots, leather vest, and belt pouches—identical in every detail to what I wore on the cobblestones.

"Incredible," I say, rotating my boots on the wooden floorboards. "What dialect was that? Ancient High Elven?"

"Meaningless gibberish," Illuna says flatly.

I blink. "Excuse me?"

"Nonsense syllables invented to manufacture an artificial sense of ritual," Illuna says.

"And the feather?"

"A focal point for your concentration," she explains. "In this domain, mental conviction dictates reality. To restore your form, you required only the unwavering belief that the manifestation would succeed. Knowing that I had already restored Leila and Hadrik gave the ritual authority in your mind. Had I instructed you to simply 'believe without doubt,' your analytical nature would have immediately interrogated the premise and sabotaged the effort."

"So simpler manifestations require only focus," I say. "What about conjuring complex matter from scratch, like Flower's palm tree and cart?"

"Flower has inhabited this plane since birth," Illuna says. "Her ethereal physiology is adapted to the medium, but more importantly, the ether rewards raw emotion over structural deduction. Flower feels with absolute intensity. This is perhaps the sole realm in existence where her lack of logical discipline serves as a decisive advantage."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Reference, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Reference)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Reference
TEXT:

"That explains the hands," I say. "When I found myself drifting helpless in the glare, the sheer panic of needing traction forced the shape into being. Emotion drove the manifestation."

"For future reference," Illuna says, "the expanse outside these walls is termed the ether. It is the final resting ground for souls severed from their earthen anchors—and the place where ethereal matter gradually breaks down and diffuses."

Flower's eyes widen. "The light out there is dead souls?"

Illuna turns a cold stare on her. "You have resided on this plane longer than I have. Did you truly never study the ecology of your own birthplace?"

"Well, yes," Flower mumbles, shifting her weight. "I just thought... maybe we were floating really close to the sun."

"There is no sun here," Illuna says flatly. "This is the ethereal plane."

"Right. Obviously. I knew that!"

"What happened to the others?" I ask, cutting in before they bicker. "Are Daren, Kate, and Melindra drifting out there? What about the king's men?"

"The exact mechanism eludes me," Illuna says, "but the Overseer channeled the resonance crystals to displace the consciousness of every living being in the sector, stripping their souls clean out of their minds."

"Kate mentioned lessathi texts describing the mind as a structure anchored in the ethereal plane," I say. "When you say displaced from their minds, you mean their ethereal anchors?"

"Precisely," Illuna says. "A mind is a fortified bastion designed to shield the fragile soul against the corrosive dissolve of the ether while maintaining the conduit to the physical flesh. This house, for example, is the interior architecture of Flower's mind."

I scan the painted walls and cluttered shelves with renewed appreciation. "So this entire structure is thought."

"We wander from the immediate crisis," Illuna says. "If the Overseer severed every anchor in the plaza, roughly a hundred bewildered souls are currently tumbling blind through the outer glare."

"Flower said displaced souls drift in a stupor," I note. "How do we track them down if they have no bodies and cannot answer a call?"

"Ask Flower," Illuna says. "She located you, Hadrik, and Leila without my assistance."

Flower straightens under our collective gaze. "When the blast hit, I felt this awful, cold squeeze in my chest—like if I didn't move immediately, everyone would slip away into nothing. And then... well..."

She trails off, tapping her chin with a furrowed brow.

"Her eyes ignited, and her aura flared into a furnace," Illuna supplies. "Within seconds, she could pinpoint any familiar consciousness across vast ethereal distances and collapse the space between them in an instant. I suspect it represents an accelerated evolutionary state unique to ethereal biology."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Collapsed, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Collapsed)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Collapsed
TEXT:

"That matches Diane," I say. "Kate's friend is an ethereal, and her eyes burned with the same vivid light when she ambushed us in the sacred woods. Flower's eyes flared red during the standoff with Eiden outside Thilias, too. Extreme emotional pressure triggers the shift."

"I would love to analyze ethereal anatomy," Flower blurts, "but what if Daren or Kate tries to draw breath like Barry almost did? I need to pull them inside before someone suffocates on air that isn't there! Be right back!"

A flare of violet brilliance wraps around her frame, and she vanishes from the room.

I glance toward Illuna. "Can you monitor the surface from here?"

"I inhabit Flower's mind," Illuna says. "I retain direct control of her earthen body."

"What is happening on the cobblestones? Are our physical bodies intact?"

"Unconscious, but breathing," Illuna reports. "The entire plaza is paralyzed. The rebel vanguard, the king, the drones—all motionless. The only entity making noise in the district is Arraka, shrieking from inside the amulet about how pathetic we are for succumbing to an elementary ethereal displacement."

"If the drones are frozen, did the Overseer misfire and displace his own consciousness?" I ask. "If he were awake, his automata would already be rounding up prisoners."

"We will have Flower scan for his signature once she returns," Illuna says. "If his synthetic soul is drifting in the ether, your hypothesis holds."

"Does Flower know the location of our individual minds?" Leila's inner voice asks, her lips remaining motionless. "I have no memory of being ejected, so I cannot provide landmarks even if she offers transport."

"She cannot track dormant mind structures," Illuna says. "Sheltering you in Flower's mind is our only viable interim measure."

"Her mind?" Hadrik blinks, looking around the wood-paneled room. "What do you mean, her mind? Isn't this just her cottage?"

"It is not a cottage," Illuna snaps, her jaw tightening. "We established this. She fashioned the interior to resemble a residence, but you stand inside the psychic architecture of her consciousness. She shapes every timber and rafter at will."

"Right, right..." Hadrik rubs his beard, groaning. "Look, I'll level with you. Ever since Flower dragged me through the front door, I've been straining so hard to keep my skull empty that I can barely process two words you're saying! If I relax for a second, my thoughts blurt across the whole room. How are you three chatting without broadcasting every stray thought in your heads?"

Leila and I exchange a brief glance.

"Illuna has centuries of discipline," I tell him. "As for Leila and me, we had a crash course in ethereal thought projection during our last encounter with the Magium crystals."

"Ah, the seasoned veterans!" Hadrik chuckles. "Care to share a few pointers with a clumsy amateur, Barry?"

choice(""Sure. Just try to empty your mind. That works best for me!"", B3-Ch09a-Pastry, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Pastry, v_b3_ch9_advice = 0)
choice(""You don't actually need to stop thinking. Just don't use words while you think."", B3-Ch09a-Pastry, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Pastry, v_b3_ch9_advice = 1)
choice(""Sorry, I'm doing it sort of instinctively so it's hard to explain."", B3-Ch09a-Pastry, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Pastry, v_b3_ch9_advice = 2)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Pastry
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_advice == 0) {
"Sure. Just try to empty your mind. That works best for me!"

"That is what I've been doing, but thinking about having an empty head just fills my head with thoughts about emptiness!" Hadrik complains. "There must be a shortcut!"

"Practice is the only shortcut," I tell him.

"I feared you would say that," Hadrik grunts. "Carry on while I wrestle my own skull. What were we dissecting before I derailed us?"

"I lost the thread," I admit. "Let us wait for Flower to return with the others."

A faint aroma of scorching sugar drifts into the parlor from the adjoining corridor.

Hadrik sniffs the air. "Does someone smell burning pastry?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_advice == 1) {
"You don't actually need to stop thinking. Just don't use words while you think."

Hadrik's brow furrows. "How do you think without words?"

"You know those split-second combat reflexes where you parry an incoming thrust before your brain can narrate the swing?" I explain. "Channel that wordless processing. Strip the verbal monologue and rely purely on intent. The room only hears what you articulate internally as speech."

Hadrik's eyes light up. "Aha! Warrior instinct over intellectual clutter! Treat the conversation like an exchange of blows!"

"Close enough," I say.

"Now we are talking!" Hadrik booms, slapping his knee. "Hadrik is back in the game! Where were we?"

"I lost the thread," I admit. "Let us wait for Flower to return with the others."

A faint aroma of scorching sugar drifts into the parlor from the adjoining corridor.

Hadrik sniffs the air. "Does someone smell burning pastry?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_advice == 2) {
"Sorry, I'm doing it sort of instinctively so it's hard to explain."

Hadrik sighs. "Trial and error it is, then. Carry on while I wrestle my own skull. What were we dissecting before I derailed us?"

"I lost the thread," I admit. "Let us wait for Flower to return with the others."

A faint aroma of scorching sugar drifts into the parlor from the adjoining corridor.

Hadrik sniffs the air. "Does someone smell burning pastry?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_premonition != 2) {
A crack of violet light illuminates the doorway. Flower materializes in the center of the room, dragging three faint, disembodied presences along with her.

"Sorry for the delay!" Flower calls out. "Daren was tumbling through a low-density pocket—took forever to hook him! Grab some chairs, everyone! Plenty of room!"

"My head..." Daren's voice groans from empty air beside the bookshelf. "The room is spinning. Is anyone else violently seasick, or is the universe singling me out?"

"I feel completely fine," Melindra's voice replies from the near armchair. "No nausea, no vertigo."

"Clear-headed on my end as well," Kate's voice adds from near the hearth.

"Nobody else is complaining," Hadrik notes. "Looks like you drew the short straw, pal."

"Wonderful," Daren mutters. "If only I could channel a basic white-magic equilibrium charm..."

"Hold on! I have medicine!" Flower scurries over to a cherry-wood nightstand and pulls out a polished wooden box.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_premonition == 2) {
A crack of violet light illuminates the doorway. Flower materializes in the center of the room, dragging two faint, disembodied presences along with her.

"Sorry for the delay!" Flower calls out. "Daren was tumbling through a low-density pocket—took forever to hook him! Grab some chairs, everyone! Plenty of room!"

"My head..." Daren's voice groans from empty air beside the bookshelf. "The room is spinning. Is anyone else violently seasick, or is the universe singling me out?"

"I feel completely fine," Melindra's voice replies from the near armchair. "No nausea, no vertigo."

"Nobody else is complaining," Hadrik notes. "Looks like you drew the short straw, pal."

"Wonderful," Daren mutters. "If only I could channel a basic white-magic equilibrium charm..."

"Hold on! I have medicine!" Flower scurries over to a cherry-wood nightstand and pulls out a polished wooden box.

}
She extracts a small white lozenge and attempts to press it into Daren's nonexistent palm. The pill drops through the air and bounces across the floorboards.

"Oops!" Flower laughs sheepishly. "Right. No hands! One second—I will convert it to raw essence!"

"You will what?" Daren asks warily.

Flower traces an intricate arc with both hands. The fallen pill dissolves into a luminous, shimmering ribbon of liquid radiance that swirls around the empty space where Daren's voice originated. She extends her palms, and the light absorbs smoothly into his invisible center.

"Better?" Flower asks.

"The dizziness..." Daren pauses, his tone turning from suspicion to relief. "The nausea is gone. Completely gone. What was that?"

"The luminous thread around your spinning core was misaligned with your—"

"Your ethereal anchor was improperly calibrated to your physical flesh," Illuna interjects smoothly. "We encountered a displaced traveler years ago with an identical affliction. Flower kept the remedy in stock."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Linked, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Linked)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Linked
TEXT:

"Improperly calibrated," Daren repeats slowly. "I assume asking why my soul lacks a clean link to my mortal flesh would be a waste of breath?"

"Beyond my diagnostic capabilities," Illuna says. "Though it likely shares a common root with the defect in your conduit to your magical plane spirit. That puzzle remains yours to solve."

Flower lets out an ecstatic squeal, clasping her hands beneath her chin as she looks across the room at the chairs and rugs.

"Visitors! Real visitors in my parlor all at once! This is the greatest day of my life! Stay right there—I'm getting the cookies!"

She bolts down the hall toward the kitchen, where the stench of charred dough is growing thick enough to taste.

#if(v_b3_ch9_premonition != 2) {
"NO!" Flower's shriek echoes from the kitchen. "My batch!"

"While she tends to her culinary disaster," Illuna says, turning back to the room, "allow me to instruct our remaining guests in the mechanics of bodily reconstruction. Conversing with disembodied drafts is growing tiresome."

Over the next several minutes, Illuna runs Daren, Melindra, and Kate through the feather ritual. By the time their limbs and clothing solidify on the rugs, Flower marches back into the room balancing a steaming silver platter piled high with golden-brown biscuits.

"Baking a fresh platter from raw flour in four minutes is an ontological impossibility," Illuna observes, narrowing her eyes. "You conjured them outright."

"I did not!" Flower protests, cheeks flushing. "I followed every single step! I just... dialed the oven heat to eleven, that's all!"

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_premonition == 2) {
"NO!" Flower's shriek echoes from the kitchen. "My batch!"

"While she tends to her culinary disaster," Illuna says, turning back to the room, "allow me to instruct our remaining guests in the mechanics of bodily reconstruction. Conversing with disembodied drafts is growing tiresome."

Over the next several minutes, Illuna runs Daren and Melindra through the feather ritual. By the time their limbs and clothing solidify on the rugs, Flower marches back into the room balancing a steaming silver platter piled high with golden-brown biscuits.

"Baking a fresh platter from raw flour in four minutes is an ontological impossibility," Illuna observes, narrowing her eyes. "You conjured them outright."

"I did not!" Flower protests, cheeks flushing. "I followed every single step! I just... dialed the oven heat to eleven, that's all!"

}
"In short, you cheated," Illuna says flatly.

"What choice did I have?" Flower sets the platter down on the oak table and begins arranging porcelain cups and saucers with rapid precision. "Leaving guests waiting on an empty stomach is unpardonable! It's bad manners!"

"Yet making me wait on your baking is perfectly acceptable?" Illuna counters.

"You live here!" Flower retorts, filling the saucers. "They are company! Company gets the good china and expedited baking! Besides, we've used the slow oven every morning for twenty years—you're used to it by now!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Decades, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Decades)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Decades
TEXT:

Hadrik rubs his temples, looking between the two of them. "You two have sat down for tea and biscuits in this room every single day for two decades?"

"Religious adherence," Illuna says. "Flower discovered a children's storybook early in our coexistence illustrating how a proper household takes afternoon refreshments together. Having lacked a biological family, she enforced the tradition with unyielding discipline."

"You could have declined," Hadrik points out as Flower bustles back toward the kitchen. "She can hardly tie you to a chair."

"Naturally I could have refused," Illuna replies, folding her arms. "Your point?"

"No point," Hadrik mumbles, scratching his beard. "Never mind."

So Illuna despises the afternoon tea routine, yet sits through it every single morning just to humor Flower. The grumpy jailer has a softer center than she admits.

Illuna's cold blue eyes snap directly to mine, her brow furrowing in a sharp glare. "Every consciousness in this room hears your thoughts, Barry. Have you forgotten already?"

"Right," I say, clearing my throat. "Unintentional broadcast. My apologies."

"Tea is served!" Flower announces, carrying in a steaming porcelain kettle and pouring fragrant, amber liquid into each of our cups.

Daren takes an experimental sip, his eyebrows lifting. "This is surprisingly rich. What blend of dried leaf is this?"

"I have no idea!" Flower says cheerfully. "I pictured the warmest, richest flavor I could imagine and willed the leaves into the strainer. I doubt the plant exists in the real world."

"And the biscuits?" Melindra asks between bites. "They taste remarkably similar to the pastry you conjure with the golden fox's food magic on the surface."

"Close cousins, but these are superior," Flower explains, beaming. "The fox's spell is wonderful, but it is bound by physical rules. Here in my mind, I can reconstruct any flavor I have ever tasted—or invent entirely new ones using raw imagination! The freedom here is limitless!"

choice("Ask Flower what is the best original food that she invented on the ethereal plane.", B3-Ch09a-Cushioned, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Cushioned, v_b3_ch9_food = 0)
choice("Ask Flower why she eats the same cookies every day if she can make any food she wants.", B3-Ch09a-Cushioned, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Cushioned, v_b3_ch9_food = 1)
choice("Ask Flower if she can replicate the biscuits from my hometown without the recipe.", B3-Ch09a-Cushioned, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Cushioned, v_b3_ch9_food = 2)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Cushioned
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_food == 0) {
"What is the single best dish you have ever concocted in the ether?" I ask Flower.

Flower taps her lips, her red eyes sparkling. "If I had to crown one... the dark chocolate sponge cake."

"Unequivocally the chocolate cake," Illuna agrees from the doorway.

"Just chocolate?" I say. "With unlimited reality-shaping at your fingertips, I expected something exotic. Leviathan marrow, crystallized star-nectar..."

"It resembles earthly chocolate only in name," Flower says, her smile widening. "Petal attempted to bake an earthen copy for my birthday, but the physical world simply lacks the base aromatics. It is simpler to show you. Taste for yourself!"

She flicks her fingers. Instantly, porcelain plates materialize on our laps, each bearing a thick, glistening slice of dark sponge cake and a silver fork.

I carve off a bite. The flavor hits the tongue with stunning complexity—deep, roasted cacao undertones layered with an intense, velvety sweetness that shifts across the palate like cooling silk. There is nothing remotely like it in any bakery across the eastern or western continents.

"Incredible," Melindra breathes, scraping her fork clean. "You can manifest this on command? With zero mana drain?"

"Whenever I want!" Flower says proudly. "No exhaustion, no limits. Seconds for everyone!"

"Before we feast," Melindra says, setting her plate aside, "can you check the plaza? What is happening on the cobblestones?"

Flower tilts her head, her gaze drifting unfocused into the rafters as her fingers weave through invisible strings.

"The drones are dead silent. Most of the vanguard is still out cold... wait. One signature is stirring. Peter is awake in the mortal world. And I feel his soul here too! He is paddling through the outer ether, hauling his squad mates toward a cluster. Look... Peter is an ethereal!"

"What about the Overseer?" Daren asks around a mouthful of cake. "He claimed to possess a manufactured soul. Can you pick up his trace in the light?"

"Yes... faint, but moving," Flower says. "He is circling in the outer expanse, searching for his mind anchor."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_food == 1) {
"A question," I say. "If you can manifest any culinary masterpiece across reality, why drink the identical tea and biscuits every single morning?"

"They are not identical!" Flower protests, her cheeks flushing. "I tweak the cinnamon notes and cardamom blends! And it's a household tradition! Is it a crime to share tea with family?"

"Not a crime," I say. "Just a curious limit on infinite power."

"I enjoy the routine," Flower says firmly. "And Petal loves it too. Right, Petal?"

"The crowning joy of my existence," Illuna says with dry irony.

"See?" Flower says, completely missing the sarcasm. "Unanimous approval! Now drink your tea before the steam dies!"

"Before we finish our cups," Melindra says, setting her saucer down, "can you check the plaza? What is happening on the cobblestones?"

Flower tilts her head, her gaze drifting unfocused into the rafters as her fingers weave through invisible strings.

"The drones are dead silent. Most of the vanguard is still out cold... wait. One signature is stirring. Peter is awake in the mortal world. And I feel his soul here too! He is paddling through the outer ether, hauling his squad mates toward a cluster. Look... Peter is an ethereal!"

"What about the Overseer?" Daren asks, chewing on a biscuit. "He claimed to possess a manufactured soul. Can you pick up his trace in the light?"

"Yes... faint, but moving," Flower says. "He is circling in the outer expanse, searching for his mind anchor."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_food == 2) {
"Can you reproduce the savory biscuits from my home village without a written recipe?" I ask.

"Let me check the memory archives," Flower says, closing her eyes. "Was the crust textured like... this?"

She extends an open palm. A golden, flaky biscuit materializes between her fingers, steam rising from the cracked butter top. I take it and break off a piece.

The flaky crumb, the pinch of mountain salt, the subtle rosemary finish—it is an exact match for the hearth ovens of my childhood.

"Spot on," I say, chewing rapidly. "Can you produce a batch?"

"A mountain of them!" Flower laughs, summoning a wide ceramic bowl heaped with warm biscuits onto the center table. "Barry's village rolls for the entire house!"

"Now that is proper trail rations," Daren says, grabbing two. "Excellently done, Flower."

"Eat as much as you like!" Flower beams. "Zero mana cost!"

"Before we clean the bowl," Melindra says, dusting crumbs from her knuckles, "can you check the plaza? What is happening on the cobblestones?"

Flower tilts her head, her gaze drifting unfocused into the rafters as her fingers weave through invisible strings.

"The drones are dead silent. Most of the vanguard is still out cold... wait. One signature is stirring. Peter is awake in the mortal world. And I feel his soul here too! He is paddling through the outer ether, hauling his squad mates toward a cluster. Look... Peter is an ethereal!"

"What about the Overseer?" Daren asks, swallowing his bite. "He claimed to possess a manufactured soul. Can you pick up his trace in the light?"

"Yes... faint, but moving," Flower says. "He is circling in the outer expanse, searching for his mind anchor."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_premonition == 2) {
"Did Kate drift into the light as well?" I ask. "I didn't see her materialize with Daren and Melindra."

"Kate was never in the ether," Flower says with absolute certainty. "Her consciousness remained anchored in her physical skull."

"The displacement pulse likely required an active nervous circuit between the soul and the waking flesh," Illuna explains. "Kate was already deeply unconscious from Albert's shock. The wave washed over her without catching traction."

"I am glad she is safe," Flower sighs, "though I really wanted to show her my toy collection."

"What about the king?" Hadrik asks. "He was thirty paces up in the storm when the pulse detonated. Did the fall break his neck?"

"I tracked his descent through Flower's eyes," Illuna says. "A kinetic absorption ward of hardened aura deployed around his body before impact. He absorbed the crater without broken bones."

"Naturally," Daren mutters. "The man has three layers of armor for every occasion."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_premonition != 2) {
"What about the king?" Hadrik asks. "He was thirty paces up in the storm when the pulse detonated. Did the fall break his neck?"

"I tracked his descent through Flower's eyes," Illuna says. "A kinetic absorption ward of hardened aura deployed around his body before impact. He absorbed the crater without broken bones."

"Naturally," Daren mutters. "The man has three layers of armor for every occasion."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Wandered, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Wandered)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Wandered
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"Flower," Melindra says, leaning forward in her chair. "You mentioned we have to navigate back to our individual mind bastions on our own. How long does memory recovery usually take?"

"Based on lost souls we have sheltered over the centuries," Illuna answers, "individual recovery varies. Typically between one to two hours."

"In two hours our throats could be slit on the cobblestones!" Daren exclaims. "Illuna, can you channel water magic through Flower's mortal body to dismantle the drones while they are frozen?"

"Out of the question," Illuna says coldly. "Flower's current evolutionary state imposes immense physiological strain on her physical vessel. Channeling high-tier elemental constructs through her frame in this state risks catastrophic organ failure. I will not jeopardize her life for battlefield expediency."

"You are right," Daren concedes, rubbing the back of his neck. "My apologies. Flower's safety outranks tactical gains."

"I feel great!" Flower insists. "I can handle a few firebal—"

"Have you mastered returning your optical array to green without triggering aura bleed?" Illuna demands.

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
"Flower," Melindra says, leaning forward in her chair. "You mentioned we have to navigate back to our individual mind bastions on our own. How long does memory recovery usually take?"

"Based on lost souls we have sheltered over the centuries," Illuna answers, "individual recovery varies. Typically between one to two hours."

"In two hours our throats could be slit on the cobblestones!" Daren exclaims. "Illuna, can you channel water magic through Flower's mortal body to bind the rebel vanguard while they are unconscious?"

"Out of the question," Illuna says coldly. "Flower's current evolutionary state imposes immense physiological strain on her physical vessel. Channeling high-tier elemental constructs through her frame in this state risks catastrophic organ failure. I will not jeopardize her life for battlefield expediency."

"You are right," Daren concedes, rubbing the back of his neck. "My apologies. Flower's safety outranks tactical gains."

"I feel great!" Flower insists. "I can handle a few firebal—"

"Have you mastered returning your optical array to green without triggering aura bleed?" Illuna demands.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_premonition == 2) {
"Working on it!" Flower mumbles.

"Then mortal-plane casting remains prohibited," Illuna decrees. "Our focus stays here."

"What if we carried the fight to the ether—" Leila's inner voice begins.

Daren jolts upright, eyes darting across the room. "Who said that?"

"I did," Leila projects, her physical lips resting in a quiet line.

"Your mouth didn't move an inch!" Daren says. "Are your thoughts broadcasting like Barry's?"

"Precisely," Leila confirms.

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_premonition != 2) {
"Working on it!" Flower mumbles.

"Then mortal-plane casting remains prohibited," Illuna decrees. "Our focus stays here."

"What if we carried the fight to the ether—" Leila's inner voice begins.

Daren jolts upright, eyes darting across the room. "Who said that?"

"I did," Leila projects, her physical lips resting in a quiet line.

"Your mouth didn't move an inch!" Daren says. "Are your thoughts broadcasting like Barry's?"

"Precisely," Leila confirms.

"Is that your inner voice, Leila?" Kate asks softly.

Leila flushes slightly. "I constructed the cadence by blending vocal traits from girls I overheard in childhood. It is an artificial construct, not my natural speech."

"It suits you," Kate says with a rare, genuine smile. "Do not hide it."

Leila offers a grateful nod.

}
"Bloody hell," Daren's voice suddenly booms across the parlor at ear-splitting volume, despite his jaw remaining clamped shut. "If I can hear Leila and Barry inside my skull, does that mean every stray thought in my head is blaring across the room right now?"

"Every syllable, Daren," Illuna says, rubbing her ear. "And you are projecting at gale-force volume. Lower your internal pitch."

"Right, sorry!" Daren's vocal cords say aloud.

An instant later, his booming internal shout returns: "Fantastic. Now I have to police every fleeting impulse so I don't narrate my private business to the entire party. Absolute nightmare."

Hadrik, Melindra, and I bite back grins. Daren catches our expressions and scowls.

"Broadcasting again?"

"At top volume," I confirm.

"Splendid," Daren mutters, sinking into his chair.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Foundation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Foundation)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Foundation
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_advice == 1 && v_b3_ch9_food == 0) {
Hadrik's booming laugh rattles the china.

"Welcome to the chorus, pal! Don't fret—Barry gave me the secret. You have to think with your warrior instincts instead of your internal monologue!"

While Hadrik drills Daren on wordless intent, the rest of us polish off our cake. Once Daren gets his volume under control, he turns back toward Leila.

"Leila, you were proposing something before I shouted over you. What was it?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_advice == 1 && v_b3_ch9_food == 1) {
