"Leila, you were proposing something before I shouted over you. What was it?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"I wanted to ask Flower if we can engage the Overseer directly in the ether," Leila projects.

"Combat in the ether is entirely possible," Flower says, "though I wouldn't advise it for beginners."

"Why not?" Hadrik asks.

"You required a structured ritual simply to manifest a ribcage ten minutes ago," Illuna says bluntly. "Entering an ethereal duel in your current state is suicidal."

"The Overseer is just as new to this plane as we are!" Hadrik argues. "He was kicked out of his core just like us. Even footing!"

"Hardly even," Illuna counters. "The Overseer designed the displacement array that cast us here. He absorbed extensive cosmological knowledge during his integration with the Magium fragment. Underestimating his ethereal manipulation would be fatal. Even Flower will be tested."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
"I wanted to ask Flower if we can engage the rebel leadership directly in the ether," Leila projects.

"Combat in the ether is entirely possible," Flower says, "though I wouldn't advise it for beginners."

"Why not?" Hadrik asks.

"You required a structured ritual simply to manifest a ribcage ten minutes ago," Illuna says bluntly. "Entering an ethereal duel in your current state is suicidal."

"The rebels are just as displaced as we are!" Hadrik argues. "They never planned for an out-of-body scrap. Even footing!"

"Hardly even," Illuna counters. "As Flower identified, Peter is an ethereal native. We have no measure of his combat prowess in this medium. If Flower faces him alone, she holds a tactical chance. If any of you accompany her, you become unshielded liabilities she must constantly defend."

}
"How does combat manifest here?" Daren asks. "Does Flower cast fire magic like on the surface?"

"This plane is constructed purely of concept and intent," Illuna explains. "Physical magic does not exist. However, the theoretical principles underlying spellcraft are fully operational. With sufficient discipline, one can project constructs that mimic magical effects with deadly precision."

I freeze, staring at her. "Did you just say that casting here requires only complete mastery of the underlying magical theory?"

"You oversimplify," Illuna says. "For an ethereal or a spirit, manifestation is instinct. For an untrained human, you must mentally calculate the vector, velocity, and density of every individual magical particle in the spell's matrix while maintaining flawless conceptual focus."

"If I know the complete matrix," I press, "the spell will ignite?"

"In theory," Illuna concedes. "Though I fail to see how—"

"Stand back," I say, stepping into the open center of the parlor. "Give me room."

choice("Attempt to cast a fireball.", B3-Ch09a-Stance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Stance, v_b3_ch9_attempt = 0)
choice("Attempt to shoot a gust of wind.", B3-Ch09a-Stance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Stance, v_b3_ch9_attempt = 1)
choice("Attempt to create a water jet.", B3-Ch09a-Stance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Stance, v_b3_ch9_attempt = 2)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Stance
TEXT:

I plant my boots shoulder-width apart, sink my weight, and thrust both palms forward.

I have dissected these matrices ten thousand times across a decade of candlelight and worn parchment. Every particle trajectory, every density gradient, every hand alignment—it is etched into my memory. In the physical realm, my lack of an internal conduit made the knowledge useless. Here, thought is the conduit.

I build the matrix in my mind.

Instantly, the air before my palms hums with volatile potential. The particles churn wildly, threatening to shear off their axes. The old panic flares, but the voice of an old hermit I studied under years ago echoes in my thoughts.

"When the flow drifts, do not wrestle it," the hermit had told me over a smoking brazier. "Forcing a drifting current only snaps the weave. Follow the deviation. Guide it gently through the arc. If your theory is flawless and your stance is true, the flow will always circle back to its true path."

#if(v_b3_ch9_attempt == 0) {
I shift my wrists, letting my palms track the widening spiral of the fire matrix. The particles wobble on the brink of collapse, then snap back into alignment along the intended axis. The moment the geometry locks, I drive all my focused intent into the nexus.

Ignite.

A roaring sphere of orange flame erupts from my palms, streaking across the parlor. It slams into the far wall and detonates in a burst of sparks and soot.

"My dragon tapestry!" Flower screams, darting across the room with a pitcher as Illuna whips a stream of water from her fingertips to douse the burning fibers.

Daren stares at my smoking palms, his jaw slack. "By the gods... Barry, how did you manifest that?"

"Years of obsession," I say, rolling my shoulders. "When you spend a decade believing that absolute theoretical mastery can bridge the gap between a mundane human and a mage, you memorize every single particle flow by heart."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_attempt == 1) {
I shift my wrists, letting my palms track the widening spiral of the wind matrix. The particles wobble on the brink of collapse, then snap back into alignment along the intended axis. The moment the geometry locks, I drive all my focused intent into the nexus.

Release.

A violent gale blasts outward from my hands, tearing across the parlor table. The porcelain vase flips, scattering water and freshly picked tulips across the rugs.

"My flowers!" Flower wails, scrambling over the floorboards to gather the wet stems.

Daren stares at my smoking palms, his jaw slack. "By the gods... Barry, how did you manifest that?"

"Years of obsession," I say, rolling my shoulders. "When you spend a decade believing that absolute theoretical mastery can bridge the gap between a mundane human and a mage, you memorize every single particle flow by heart."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_attempt == 2) {
I shift my wrists, letting my palms track the widening spiral of the water matrix. The particles wobble on the brink of collapse, then snap back into alignment along the intended axis. The moment the geometry locks, I drive all my focused intent into the nexus.

Surge.

A pressurized torrent of water jets from my hands, striking the side table like a battering ram. The vase shatters into porcelain shards against the wall, flooding the baseboards.

"My vase!" Flower shrieks, dropping to her knees to gather the wet fragments.

Daren stares at my wet palms, his jaw slack. "By the gods... Barry, how did you manifest that?"

"Years of obsession," I say, rolling my shoulders. "When you spend a decade believing that absolute theoretical mastery can bridge the gap between a mundane human and a mage, you memorize every single particle flow by heart."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Lunatic, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Lunatic)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Lunatic
TEXT:

Illuna steps forward, studying me with sharp intensity. "Why did you follow the drifting flow instead of forcing a manual correction? That defies standard combat doctrine."

"An old hermit taught me," I say. "If the underlying matrix is mathematically sound, a fluctuating current will naturally self-correct along the path of least resistance."

"That principle functions only if you are entirely certain you made zero errors in calculating the position of millions of sub-particles," Illuna points out. "You harbored no doubt whatsoever?"

"The macro-patterns are predictable once you understand the harmonic ratios," I tell her. "And no, I had no doubts. I spent years drilling these forms in my backyard while the neighbors crossed the street to avoid the lunatic waving his arms at thin air. I am long past doubting my geometry."

Illuna stares at me in silence for several seconds.

"Arraka gave me the exact same justification centuries ago," she murmurs. "When I asked why she yielded to a destabilized weave, she told me she had outgrown the capacity for mathematical error. I finally comprehend why she is so relentless in her attempts to unlock your stat device."

She pauses, tapping her chin.

"How many complete matrices have you memorized?"

"A core handful across each elemental school," I say. "Plus the flight current matrix Melindra gave me, which I had to calculate manually during the skeleton ambush. Beyond those, I know the theory, but I need the particle vectors mapped."

#if(v_b3_ch9_premonition == 2) {
"Time is against us," Illuna says. "Focus on a single element if you intend to be useful in combat. You may study water with me, fire with Flower, or air with Melindra. Choose."

}
#if(v_b3_ch9_premonition != 2) {
"Time is against us," Illuna says. "Focus on a single element if you intend to be useful in combat. You may study water with me, fire with Flower, air with Melindra, or ice with Kate. Choose."

}
choice("Fire", B3-Ch09a-Fire, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Fire, v_b3_ch9_element = 0)
choice("Water", B3-Ch09a-Water, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Water, v_b3_ch9_element = 1)
choice("Air", B3-Ch09a-Air, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Air, v_b3_ch9_element = 2)
choice("Ice", B3-Ch09a-Ice, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Ice, v_b3_ch9_element = 3) if (v_b3_ch9_premonition != 2)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Fire
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch9_attempt == 0) {
"If we only have time for one school, fire is my choice," I say.

"Logical," Illuna says. "Flower, over here. Barry requires—Flower, what in the world are you doing?"

"Restoring the tapestry!" Flower calls out from the far wall, a horsehair paintbrush hovering in her hand.

"We have no time for manual restorations," Illuna says sharply. "You insisted that our guest comes first, and our guest requires combat instruction. Revert the fibers instantly."

"Fine, fine..." Flower sighs. She flicks her wrist, and the scorched threads snap back into vibrant, unburnt wool.

She bounds across the parlor, skidding to a halt before me.

"Alright, Barry! What are we casting?"

I spend ten minutes demonstrating the basic flame matrices I know, while Flower corrects the angle of my elbows and the rotation of my wrists. When we exhaust my catalog, she tries to explain her personal repertoire—with mixed results.

"The easiest way to channel this arc is to start with a backflip and plant on your left hand!" Flower chirps. "Watch!"

}
