"No acrobatics," I say quickly. "I believe your technique works, but I need forms that don't require Olympic gymnastics."

"Right, right!" Flower says, tapping her chin. "Sorry! I'm trying to translate this into language that doesn't sound like elementalist instinct."

"What do you mean?"

"When I coached Kate on her freezing beam yesterday," Flower explains, "I just told her to hollow out the core pressure and flare the perimeter chill, and her body knew what to do. You don't have that muscle memory because you learned everything from parchment."

"I know the sensation," I say. "When my device unlocked my magic against Tyrath and the stillwater skeletons, it felt less like plotting coordinates and more like sculpting a living current from my chest. But without the device feeding me instinct, I have to rely on raw geometry."

"Exactly!" Flower says. "Elementalists bend their element on a whim without following a blueprint. I improvise constantly, which makes it nearly impossible to teach you step by step."

"You remember the tunnel trap we disarmed under Ollendor?" I remind her. "You thought you invented a unique dispel matrix, and it turned out an ancient master mapped the exact same flow centuries ago. Let me describe the standard fire matrices from the academy texts, and you tell me which ones match your instincts."

"Now that makes sense!" Flower grins. "Fire away!"

For forty minutes, we work through text classifications. Whenever she recognizes an arc, I map her descriptions to particle flows and rotational speeds. Knowing the fundamental grammar of spellcraft allows me to extrapolate missing variables with high precision.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Afford, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Afford)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Water
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"If we only have time for one school, water is my choice," I say.

"Acceptable," Illuna says, stepping into the center of the room. "Show me the matrices you have mapped, and we will build from there."

I spend several minutes demonstrating my catalog of basic water spells. Illuna provides crisp adjustments to my weight distribution and wrist elevation, refining the flow. Once we establish my baseline, she pauses in calculation.

"Assuming we have under an hour before the Overseer navigates back to his core... which branches do you estimate you can master?"

"If we move fast," I estimate, "perhaps the opening sections of an introductory hydromancy treatise."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
"If we only have time for one school, water is my choice," I say.

"Acceptable," Illuna says, stepping into the center of the room. "Show me the matrices you have mapped, and we will build from there."

I spend several minutes demonstrating my catalog of basic water spells. Illuna provides crisp adjustments to my weight distribution and wrist elevation, refining the flow. Once we establish my baseline, she pauses in calculation.

"Assuming we have under an hour before the rebels navigate back to their mind bastions... which branches do you estimate you can master?"

"If we move fast," I estimate, "perhaps the opening sections of an introductory hydromancy treatise."

}
"A refreshingly grounded assessment," Illuna says, "though fundamentally flawed."

"How so?"

"Manual classifications are deceptive," Illuna explains. "A spell cataloged as 'elementary' often demands high mechanical stability under pressure. If a technique fails one time in five under combat pressure, it is a liability, regardless of its simplicity. You require high-reliability constructs."

"Reliability over breadth," I say, nodding. "A tight arsenal I can execute in the dark without misfiring. Do you have a curated sequence?"

"I do," Illuna says. "If you maintain absolute focus, you can achieve combat proficiency across five core matrices before our window closes. Begin."

For forty minutes, Illuna drills me through her tactical selection. Her instructions are precise, economical, and grounded in structural theory. Under her guidance, the particle arcs lock into place with remarkable consistency. Choosing her as an instructor was the right call.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Afford, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Afford)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Air
TEXT:

"If we only have time for one school, air is my choice," I say.

Melindra's eyebrows shoot up. "You want aeromancy lessons from me? Knowing my power regulation is a disaster?"

"Your magnitude control is messy," I counter. "Your vector geometry is flawless. You understand the currents better than anyone in the room."

Melindra shrugs, resting her hands on her hips. "If you think your book lore can filter out my bad habits, be my guest. Just don't complain when we hit a wall."

I run through my standard wind matrices, demonstrating the basic drafts and deflection shields. Melindra watches critically, offering sharp adjustments to my follow-through. When she attempts to introduce a high-velocity gale construct, however, the translation stalls.

"Hold on," Melindra mutters after my third attempt fizzles into a weak puff of breeze. "Let me think how to phrase this for someone who learned magic from diagrams instead of living through it."

"Trial and error works fine if we move quickly," I tell her. "How do you internalize a new wind weave?"

"I find the harmonic frequency of the mana pooling in my chest," Melindra explains. "I tune my intent to the vibration. Does that make any sense to you?"

"That is elemental resonance," I say. "I felt that during my fight with Tyrath while the stat device was active. But I have no internal reservoir here to tune. What about your physical cues? Where do your eyes track when you initiate the cast? An elementalist's gaze always leads the primary vector."

Melindra closes her eyes, falling into a light stance as she visualizes the spell. "Upper left quadrant... sweeping downward along the rib line... snapping hard back to the upper right."

"Got it," I say. "That's a standard spiral-shear pattern. I know where the tail ends. Watch my form."

I initiate the matrix, tracking the eye vector with my palms. The air shears cleanly into a concentrated gust that ruffles the parlor curtains.

We use the gaze-tracking method to unlock four more constructs over the next forty minutes, the time slipping away as the weaves lock into memory.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Afford, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Afford)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Ice
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch9_moment,1)
achievement("An introspective moment",v_ac_b3_ch9_moment)
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"If we only have time for one school, ice is my choice," I say.

"Your student awaits, Kate," Illuna says as Kate steps forward. "Make efficient use of the hour before the Overseer recovers his core."

"Never imagined I would be drilling you in cryomancy," Kate says, stopping three paces away.

"Things change," I reply with a grin. "Keep slacking off and I might be critiquing your frost walls by next month."

"Is that a challenge?" Kate crosses her arms, raising an eyebrow. "Let us see if your casting matches your arrogance. Assume your stance. Drop the temperature in this quadrant ten degrees in thirty seconds."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
"If we only have time for one school, ice is my choice," I say.

"Your student awaits, Kate," Illuna says as Kate steps forward. "Make efficient use of the hour before the rebels recover their anchors."

"Never imagined I would be drilling you in cryomancy," Kate says, stopping three paces away.

"Things change," I reply with a grin. "Keep slacking off and I might be critiquing your frost walls by next month."

"Is that a challenge?" Kate crosses her arms, raising an eyebrow. "Let us see if your casting matches your arrogance. Assume your stance. Drop the temperature in this quadrant ten degrees in thirty seconds."

}
I run through my baseline frost spells, while Kate provides crisp, demanding corrections on my wrist rotation and balance. From there, she introduces several intermediate constructs from manuals I studied years ago. Her instruction is sharp, demanding, and stripped of wasted words, accelerating the learning curve dramatically.

"How does it feel?" Kate asks quietly as I drill the wrist snaps for an ice beam.

"How does what feel?"

"Achieving your lifelong ambition in a realm of thought."

I lower my hands, looking at the pale frost coating my knuckles.

"It feels... earned," I say after a moment. "I had magic handed to me through the stat booster against Tyrath and the stillwaters, but that was a machine doing the work. This is my own study. A decade of sleepless nights, memorizing particle grids by candlelight while people called me a fool. Even if this is just conceptual manipulation in an ethereal mind, it proves the theory was sound all along. I intend to savor every second."

Kate studies me, a soft, rare smile touching her lips. "I am glad. Now fix your elbow. Your posture is atrocious, and your beam will shatter on the first stone wall."

"Yes, ma'am," I say, snapping back into form.

We spend the remaining forty minutes refining the beam until the frost crystallizes with razor sharpness.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch09a-Afford, v_current_scene = B3-Ch09a-Afford)


ID: B3-Ch09a-Afford
TEXT:

Midway through our practice, Flower freezes. Her gaze locks onto the wooden wall with sharp, distant focus, peering straight through the psychic timber into the outer glare.

Illuna catches the shift immediately. "What do you sense?"

"Movement," Flower reports, her crimson eyes narrowing. "Peter and the Overseer are navigating the ether. They haven't crossed paths yet, but their trajectories intersect. And Peter has the king's soul in tow—they're moving as a pair."

Across the parlor, Daren grunts in frustration, sweat beading on his forehead as he attempts to force a white-light ward into existence. His construct flickers and dies. Hadrik and Melindra have long since abandoned the effort, lounging by the tea table. In this house, only Flower, Illuna, and I possess functional combat casting.

"Our window is closing," Illuna declares. "If we intend to contest the ether, we strike before their vanguard organizes."

"Are you coming with us?" Flower asks.

"I must anchor here," Illuna says. "If we both vacate your consciousness, Flower's earthen frame is left completely unpiloted and defenseless. Barry and Flower must deploy alone."

"Give me two more minutes!" Daren pleads, shaking his hands. "I am close! If I crack the matrix, I can provide healing on the field!"

"You have claimed two more minutes for the last half-hour," Illuna says bluntly. "The probability of a breakthrough is negligible. If your breakthrough occurs, Flower and I share a telepathic conduit through our fused soul. I can relay a summons, and Flower will extract you from here."

"You maintain a constant telepathic link?" Leila's inner voice asks. "Do you hear each other's conscious thoughts at all times?"

"No more than I hear yours while seated at this table," Illuna clarifies. "As long as we maintain discipline and avoid verbalizing internal thought, our minds remain private."

#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 0) {
"Understood," Leila projects.

"Ready, Barry?" Flower asks as I weave Melindra's flight currents around my limbs, locking the updraft into position. "Where do we drop? We can ambush the Overseer directly, or intercept Peter and attempt to forge an alliance. Peter might be a clumsy liability in the ether, but an extra pair of hands could tip the scale."

}
#if(v_b3_ch8_overseer2 == 1) {
"Understood," Leila projects.

"Ready, Barry?" Flower asks as I weave Melindra's flight currents around my limbs, locking the updraft into position. "Where do we drop? We can ambush Peter directly, or intercept the Overseer and attempt to forge an alliance. The machine might be a clumsy liability in the ether, but an extra pair of hands could tip the scale."

}
